  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday through Tuesday)\nIssued at 123 PM CDT Wed Sep 16 2026\n\nWarm and relatively dry weather closes the end of this week, \nbefore rain chances increase this weekend. Mid to upper ridging \ncontinues to expand west Friday through Saturday from the \nsoutheast US, although flattening of the ridge will persist from \nincreased troughing over the western CONUS. The accompanying large\nscale sinking and drying motion will maintain near zero rain \nchances across the area, apart from low to medium (25% to 35%) \nshower/storm chances over the northwest portions of the SE NM \nplains through Culberson County Friday evening. Troughing \nflattening the ridge keeps highs in the 85F-95F range, 100F-105F \nalong the Rio Grande, with lows still largely falling into the \n65F-75F range, 60F-65F range Marfa Plateau and Lower Trans Pecos. \nTroughing starts to develop east over the Rockies and Central \nGreat Plains this weekend, further flattening the ridge and \nproviding increasing lift and moisture convergence aloft for \nhigher rain chances. Additionally, a cold front near the surface \nwill attempt to progress southeast over the area in association \nwith this mid to upper trough, although highs and lows will remain\nsimilar to the previous day. PoPs increase above 35% for most of \nthe area northwest of Terrell County into the southeast Permian \nBasin late Saturday afternoon through Sunday morning, then across \nthe Big Bend into most of the Permian Basin Sunday afternoon \nthrough early Monday morning. \n\nUncertainty remains high in the long term regarding temperatures \nand position of a cold front that will attempt to develop \nsoutheast through the area late weekend through early next week. \nHighs early next week continue to trend up from previous runs. \nRather than falling below 85F, highs in most recent runs largely \nremain in the 85F-95F range, 95F-100F for usual warmer spots of \nthe Rio Grande Basin, and 75F-80F higher elevations. Model frontal\nanalyses indicate the cold front may not be able to clear the \narea, with no significant northerly/northeasterly wind shift that \nwould indicate a frontal passage. This is likely to maintain an \nunsettled weather pattern extending into next week. By the middle \nof next week, ensembles indicate a 35% to 55% chance of rainfall \ntotals at least 0.25\" to 0.50\" from the Marfa Plateau into SE NM \nplains and northern Permian Basin, with a signal for another round\nof potentially heavy rain from the middle to late portion of next\nweek with higher end percentile rainfall at least 1\". This still \ncould change, and we will continue to monitor trends and timing \nfor messaging any possible flash flooding risk. 75th and higher \npercentile shear also gradually increases above 25 knots by middle\nto late next week, so we will also have to watch for any severe \nstorm risk, especially during this portion of the year as we head \ninto the secondary autumn severe weather season.",
